Clostridioides difficile Infection (CDI)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Foul-smelling watery diarrhea, Recent antibiotic exposure, Out-of-proportion leukocytosis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-012
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of relentless, foul-smelling diarrhea with marked lethargy following a recent course of antibiotics, requiring urgent after-hours assessment for severe dehydration and evolving sepsis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute onset of severe, frequent watery diarrhea resulting in tachycardia and dizziness upon standing. The patient's recent antibiotic exposure raised immediate suspicion for fulminant colitis. This required an urgent home assessment to evaluate intravascular volume status and prevent progression to toxic megacolon or severe shock prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Constant, foul-smelling watery diarrhea and stomach cramps. Patient states, I can't leave the toilet, the smell is horrendous, and I feel completely washed out and dizzy.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began abruptly 24 hours ago, consisting of acute onset frequent, loose, and entirely watery stools. The patient describes the odor as distinctively foul-smelling. There is a prominent recent history of broad-spectrum antibiotic use (Amoxicillin/Clavulanate) completed three days ago for a sinus infection. The patient reports severe, crampy lower abdominal pain preceding each bowel movement. Nausea and profound anorexia are present, severely limiting oral fluid intake. A low-grade fever has developed over the past few hours. The patient reports feeling lightheaded and faint upon standing, indicating evolving intravascular volume depletion.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe fatigue and orthostatic dizziness, heavily reliant on spouse for fluid administration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of profound hypovolemia; spouse is providing continuous care and monitoring.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78.0 Kg
General: Appears pale, acutely fatigued, diaphoretic, and clinically dehydrated.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is pronounced. Capillary refill is delayed (3-4 seconds). Postural blood pressure drop is clinically evident upon assisted standing. Respiratory: Lungs are clear. Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is mildly distended with diffuse lower abdominal tenderness and hyperactive bowel sounds. There is no rigid guarding or rebound tenderness at this stage to suggest perforation. Integumentary: The diagnostic triad is clinically suspected: the patient exhibits foul-smelling watery diarrhea and has confirmed recent antibiotic exposure. Examination reveals decreased skin turgor and dry mucous membranes. While out-of-proportion leukocytosis is an expected laboratory finding, the clinical severity correlates with this high-risk infectious process.
Environmental Observations: Strong, distinct odor in the vicinity of the bathroom. Spouse has initiated rigorous hygiene protocols.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Recent cessation of Amoxicillin/Clavulanate confirmed. No current antidiarrheal use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Clostridioides difficile Infection (CDI) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Viral Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct timing immediately following broad-spectrum antibiotic exposure, the specific malodorous nature of the stool, and the severity of systemic toxicity.

Ischemic Colitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset of watery diarrhea rather than sudden, severe, bloody diarrhea with acute localized ischemic pain.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No prior history of chronic alternating bowel habits, and symptoms correlate perfectly with recent antibiotic cessation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits severe gastrointestinal fluid loss leading to clinical hypovolemia, characterized by tachycardia, postural dizziness, and dry mucous membranes. The timeline of symptoms immediately following broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy is pathognomonic for a C. difficile overgrowth. Delaying care until the next routine appointment posed an unacceptable risk of the patient progressing to severe septic shock, acute kidney injury, or toxic megacolon. Urgent after-hours assessment confirmed the diagnosis clinically, ruled out an immediate surgical abdomen, and allowed for the initiation of targeted, high-volume oral rehydration therapy and an explicit directive to avoid antimotility agents while awaiting urgent morning pathology. The expected investigation results include a positive stool assay for C. difficile toxins A and B or detection of toxigenic C. difficile genes via PCR, coupled with significant leukocytosis, that would assist in confirming the diagnosis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated aggressive oral rehydration therapy. Strictly prohibited the use of Loperamide or any antimotility agents. Prescribed Paracetamol 1000mg PO for fever and abdominal discomfort. Arranged for an urgent morning stool specimen collection.
Positive Behavioral Change: To replace the massive fluid volume you are losing, commit to drinking 250ml of an oral rehydration solution after every single bout of diarrhea. Keep a clear visual tally sheet next to your bed. Marking off each finished glass will provide a tangible sense of accomplishment and directly correlate with a reduction in your dizziness, rewarding your compliance with improved physical stability.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review tomorrow morning to review stool assay results and commence targeted antibiotic therapy (e.g., oral Vancomycin or Fidaxomicin).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if your abdomen becomes severely swollen and rock-hard, if your abdominal pain becomes constant and agonizing, or if you become confused or unable to wake up.

